// Accepted deviations from SonarQube Cloud's findings.
//
// This is the one place a Sonar finding is argued with. A fix session that thinks a finding
// is wrong adds an entry here with a reason; it does not sprinkle // NOSONAR comments or
// disable an analyzer. See docs/adr/0016-sonar-issue-triage.md.
//
// Entry shape:
//   rule     required. The full Sonar rule key, e.g. "csharpsquid:S2178".
//   verdict  "fix" (default), "accept" (never file it), or "defer" (not now).
//   paths    optional globs narrowing the entry. "**" crosses directories, "*" does not.
//   message  optional regex the finding's message must match.
//   reason   required for accept and defer. A reason appealing to a repo-wide design
//            choice must cite an ADR or a ticket, so review can check it.
//
// A verdict of accept or defer without a reason is a hard error: that is how a backlog
// quietly rots. Nothing here is hidden - every run prints the suppressed count by rule.
{
  "rules": [
    {
      "rule": "csharpsquid:S2178",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "paths": ["src/Equiv.Core/**"],
      "reason": "Non-short-circuit & on bools is deliberate here: it keeps each field to one branch, which the 100% branch-coverage gate requires. Rewriting to && doubles the branches per field and the gate then cannot be met. Rationale and the original Sonar report: src/Equiv.Core/Ir/IrEquality.cs and docs/tickets/M1-003-verdicts-matching-config.md."
    },
    {
      "rule": "csharpsquid:S2094",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "paths": ["**/AssemblyMarker.cs"],
      "reason": "These types are empty on purpose: they exist only so the ArchUnitNET rules in tests/Equiv.Tests.Architecture can resolve each src/ assembly (M0-003). Giving them members or turning them into interfaces would defeat the point."
    },
    {
      "rule": "csharpsquid:S8969",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "paths": ["src/Equiv.Frontend.CSharp/Lowering/IrLowerer.cs"],
      "reason": "False positive from GH-39: Sonar's finding at IrLowerer.Match claims the null-forgiving operator on pattern.Value.Type! is redundant, but pattern.Value.Type is a different property than the type-pattern's own Value.Type bound by the enclosing case (same member name, unrelated operations). Removing the ! makes Roslyn's nullable flow analysis raise CS8604, which fails the build under warnings-as-errors -- the compiler does not, in fact, already know this is non-null."
    }
  ]
}
